THE ONE FAILING CELL IS AN OVER-ASSERTION ON PROSE, AND GEMINI NAMED THE PRINCIPLE WITHOUT
HAVING SEEN THE MATRIX. or-nemotron's suite on glm:

    assertion failed: reason.contains("fails against merged code")

My clause 3 pins that `reason` is non-empty and says the test fails against merged code. It does
not fix the wording, and the field is documented as prose "for the bead body". or-nemotron
asserts the exact phrase; a rival writing "does not pass against the merged reference" satisfies
the clause and fails the test.

gemini, reviewing glm:

  "Testing `reason` via `lower.contains(\"fail\") && lower.contains(\"merged\")` properly checks
   the semantic requirement of Clause 3 without asserting on fragile, exact string formatting
   that would fail against rival implementations."

That is the same defect glm reported in gemini on verify-zero one task ago, and the same one I
filed after suite-verdict. Third occurrence; vetoed here, and the spec wording is the cause.

WHAT ELSE THE CRITICS FOUND.

glm on or-nemotron: "Clause 7's 'say why' is discharged in the wrong place. The spec says to say
why a reason cannot be empty 'rather than guarding against it'. The public doc on both `reason`
fields says only 'Never empty.'; the actual justification ... lives in a comment inside
`#[cfg(test)]`, invisible to rustdoc and to a reader of the public API." Real, and precisely the
kind of thing no count shows.

or-nemotron on gemini: NO MATERIAL DEFECTS FOUND. The harder answer, and the record supports it
-- nothing else in the field found anything against gemini either.

gemini on glm also noted the cost of the API I fixed: `decide`'s signature takes no caller
diagnostics, so every `reason` must be a static literal and "downstream bead formatters cannot
embed instance-specific failure traces without an API revision". It judged respecting the fixed
signature correct anyway. Worth carrying forward when something actually files these beads.

DECISION. All three pinned the six-cell table in the doc, as asked. gemini on 15 tests to 12 and
11, on 49 module-doc lines to 29 and 10, on surviving every suite, and on being the only arm no
critic found a defect in.

What this buys: when a critic finds a real defect in the arm that WINS, the finding stops
vanishing. Escalation currently writes the test, watches it fail because the defect is real and
unfixed, and retracts it -- so the better the critic, the more certainly its finding is lost.
`FileAsKnownDefect` names that case, and `Unverifiable` keeps a veto that never ran from being
mistaken for one that passed.

1472 core tests, clippy 8.
